2|0@cordcutters^1440352525#cuczo35:totodee$:Whether cord cutting is cost effective or not depends on a lot of factors including where you live. In some areas Internet plus cable TV costs only slightly more than Internet alone. But in other areas you can get good stand alone Internet for a lot less than Internet plus cable or satellite. I am lucky enough to be in one of those areas but not everyone is so lucky. And then I am also lucky to be in an area where I can get a lot of OTA channels. And not everyone has that advantage as well. So what might be a good deal for me might not be for someone else. And of course price is not the only factor for some. Some are so sick and tired of cable or satellite that they cancel it whether it saves them any money or not.
1|0@cordcutters^1440350426#cucyimp:Parade_Precipitation$:just use free streaming sites. make sure you got your adblocker plus up and running though.
1|0@cordcutters^1440350490#cucyjwk:therealjerrystaute$:Charter's charged us $60 for just internet ever since we cancelled their TV service around 3-4 years ago. But we presently have close to 200 TV channels, and only pay about an additional $8 a month atop the Charter bill. Around 24 of those channels are free OTA channels, that include all the major broadcast networks, plus local affiliate subsidiary channels, some independent channels, and some shopping channels. We're rural, in a terrible spot for an antenna, and so had to put [a high level of effort and investment](http://www.jmooneyham.com/how-we-got-over-the-air-hdtv-in-a-fringe-reception-area.html) into that to get it to work. But many people in the USA could get as many OTA channels as we do with a dirt cheap set of rabbit ears sitting on a table beside their flat screen. Another 150+ channels are carefully chosen free channels on our Roku 2 XS boxes ([here's the list of them](http://www.jmooneyham.com/best-free-roku-tv-channels-2015.html)). Lastly, the only paid TV service we have is Netflix. We also have both wired and wireless net access in our house, with one Windows 7 PC, an iMac, several tablets, and several smartphones, which can be used to get additional video/audio options not available via the antenna, Rokus, or Netflix. We basically have more TV options than we can watch, and need never ever watch a rerun of anything ever again, unless we just really, really want to.
