1|0@cordcutters^1440349423#cucxypi:Geoff-Vader$:I gladly paid more to go with DirecTV and Dish over the last 8 or so years precisely because I could not tolerate the experience that was Charter TV. The picture quality was actually quite good. But I simply could not tolerate a circa-1998 experience when it came to the software/hardware. And I've tried on 3 different occasions over the years to get a Tivo working with a cablecard on their service only to be met with constant headaches that sent me running back to satellite. Meanwhile being on Direct and Dish was utterly fantastic and has been the biggest reason I've dragged my feet on cord-cutting.
0|0@cordcutters^1440347343#cucwu5l:ZippyTheChicken$:i pay $40 a month for 3mbit from comcast I bought a Ceton 4 tuner off ebay for $50 I am compiling my own library of content so the only time I will need online content is for something I can't get off an antenna and I don't have in my archive My primary reason isn't a political statement like many ... its purely economics so I will never be buying into Netflix or Pay Services once i cut them out of my cable bill... i can do without game of throwns .. i have never seen it anyway.. And I won't be torrenting.. thats just a bit scary when you realize that .flv files can have embeded commands that give root and a lot of those connections are in countries we are at war with ... who are complicit with people we are at war with.. or if not they are from people with unscrupulous morals... not to mention i don't want my broadband cut off because I download an episode of some lame ass show or a movie.. and heres a clue not only are rates going up the caps they are introducing will have you paying per Gig of download in the future so if you are wasting your subscriptions to those services to watch something thats not unique .. you might want to get cable back for 6 months and fill a few drives full of tv shows.
2|0@cordcutters^1440347699#cucx0m0:linuxwes$:In addition to points other are making, your statement "more than I ever did for cable" suggests you are comparing what you paid for cable years ago to what you are paying for internet today. Most likely the TV prices have also risen substantially in that time. It's actually pretty hard to do a fair price comparison between cable TV and cordcutting TV because they offer such different access to content. Cordcutting is almost always on demand, so you need a DVR included in the cable price. A lot of it comes down to what works best for your situation and preferences. I know when I ran the numbers several years back I ended up saving $50/mo and getting better access to more of the kinds of content that mattered to me, but everyone's situation is different.
1|0@cordcutters^1440424538#cudvicp:Hado_Ken$:Ditto this comment. Only 3 years ago, we were paying ~$150 for all of our services. Not promotional rate either, just regular prices. Now? Same services, plus them tacking on a cable box rental to every TV you want to watch on, sent us north of $210 for the exact same service we used to have. Their price spikes and forced box rentals were the whole reason that we started investigating cord-cutting, so they only have themselves to blame.
