2|0@cordcutters^1440344868#cucvl2h:totally_rocks$:Pay as you go, with no data, and we buy our phones outright, typically used. I've yet to pay more than $100 for a cell phone. Rogers has been trying to get us to switch to prepaid for years. Last month they called me and offered me a $25/month plan. Apparently that was going to get me 200 day time minutes, free evenings and weekends after 6pm, and unlimited messaging. (More than I needed, by a long shot honestly) I asked if that was all inclusive, taxes, systems access fees, everything, they said yes. I got my first bill on Thursday, it was $31 and change. I switched back. I average about $15-$20 a month, my wife a little less. We just don't use our phones much. I do travel some times, as I'm in the Navy, but I just pick up a sim card, or use Ooma's mobile app if I can find decent wifi, when I get where I'm going. (I've tried skype, I'm willing to pay to not have to use that shit.)
2|0@cordcutters^1440346774#cucwjhn:nothinbuttherain$:If you aren't a heavy mobile data user, that's really easy to do with Ting, for example. 6 bucks a month per line, then pay for what you use. My wife's iphone rarely costs us more than 26 bucks a month (lowest bill has been 22 I think), and adding another line would likely be less than 2x that, because their "buckets" for billing are shared among all lines on the account. The only "downside" is that instead of paying 2000 bucks for your subsidized phone over the course of your 2 year contract from some other carrier, you need to outright buy a phone (you don't have to buy it from Ting) which, if you want a current gen iphone is going to be a few hundred bucks even used. We snatched up a like-new iphone 5 for my wife for about $250 shortly after the 6 came out, and have been super happy with Ting. (Not that I'm at all an Apple guy, it's just a handy example.)
1|0@cordcutters^1440374939#cudchv1:4d2$:$4/month VOIP sounds great, what is that about?
1|0@cordcutters^1440376532#cuddgjo:totally_rocks$:http://www.ooma.com/savings/ The only thing they charge, after the purchase price of the device, is the actual tax that is required in your area. I went with ooma because I read a bunch of reviews that said they would work well with a monitored alarm. But look at other voip providers as well if that's not a concern. The system wouldn't play well with my alarm monitoring company's old as hell equipment, but I kept trying to find away to make it work and eventually I came across eyezon.com. In the process of switching to voip I was also able to drop my alarm monitoring by $15 a month. Win-win!
